{"round": 1, "phase": "full", "verdict": "ISSUES_FOUND", "blocking": true, "verifier_model": "gpt-5.4", "verifier_provider": "openai", "orchestrator_provider": "anthropic", "findings": [{"description": "Same-step `workflow enter` clears the cap state and reopens fresh review rounds on the same step.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "A target reaches the cap on `plan` or `decompose`, the author edits the artifact, then runs `workflow enter <current-step>` before `workflow review` again. That path is accepted, and it zeroes `reviewRounds` and `lastLiveReview`, so the same step can spend another full set of vendor rounds. This is probable because there is no other CLI path to reopen review of that same step after edits without moving backward or forward, so `enter` is the obvious command a user will try.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/workflow.py"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {"description": "`workflow review` and `workflow status` resolve the round cap from the caller's current directory, not the supplied workspace root.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "A repository sets `local-overrides.yaml` to `verification.settings.max_rounds: 1`, and an operator or extension invokes `workflow status` or `workflow review --workspace-root <repo>` from some other working directory. `review_cap()` ignores `<repo>` and loads config via `Path.cwd()`, so enforcement and status/projection can use a different cap (typically the bundled default 3 or another repo's override), allowing extra vendor rounds and reporting the wrong bound. This is probable because the product explicitly supports `--workspace-root` and `project(root)` as first-class entrypoints.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/config.py", "ai_router/config.py"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {